In this lesson we'll look at three different ways to write the same quadratic function, and why each one is useful. A quadratic is any function where you take x, square it, and add a couple of other terms, and its graph is a smooth U-shape called a parabola. The first way to write it is the general form, which is just the x-squared term, the x term, and the constant all added together. The handy thing here is that the constant tells you straight away where the graph crosses the y-axis. If the number in front of the x-squared is positive, the parabola opens upward, and if it's negative, it opens downward. Next, we have the turning-point form. This version lets you read off the very top or very bottom of the parabola, called the turning point, and the vertical line of symmetry that cuts through it. So it tells you where the maximum or minimum sits. Finally, there's the factored form. This one shows you exactly where the parabola crosses the x-axis, and the axis of symmetry sits right in the middle between those two crossing points. Now let's see it in action.